    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Huntington’s disease (HD) is a devastating neurodegenerative condition that results in uncontrolled movements, deterioration of intellectual capabilities and emotional instability. HD is classified as an autosomal dominant, tri-nucleotide repeat disorder caused by abnormal expansion of “CAG” repeats in the HTT gene on chromosome 4. HTT codes for the protein huntingtin (HTT), which is linked to diverse cellular functions. Mutant HTT tends to misfold and form protein aggregates. Mutant HTT toxicity affects the entire brain, but it is particularly devastating within the corticostriatal axis. Although the exact mechanisms are currently debated, mutant HTT leads to a decrease in the transcription and delivery of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) from the cortex to the striatum. BDNF plays a fundamental role in the survival and terminal differentiation of medium spiny neurons (MSNs) in the striatum. I hypothesize that bypassing the defective mechanism of BDNF transcription and delivery through the use of virus-induced BDNF expression in the striatum and cortex can rescue MSNs. In this thesis, I produce proBDNF viruses and assess their effects in-vitro through a striatal autocrine pathway and a corticostriatal paracrine pathway. The paracrine pathway requires a reconstruction of the corticostriatal axis that is only possible with the novel microfluidic co-culture platform that is employed in this study. The ability to recreate and manipulate the in-vivo corticostriatal architecture in a cheap, replicable in-vitro platform is extremely powerful in pushing the frontlines for HD therapeutics. This thesis strives to unite the promising strategy of BDNF viral gene therapy with an innovative research platform: the microfluidic cell co-culture. Huntington’s disease does not currently have a cure, giving great urgency to the search for therapies.",
